/* eslint-disable */
// Product: the flagship (roadmap v3 §5, the `mc` track, 1.5 h): 78 slots. Mon–Fri: the 59 flagship steps (FLAGSHIP, in
// order) and the 2 take-homes (TAKE_HOMES, Mon–Wed of weeks 7 and 10). Saturdays: the 13 LLD sessions (LLD).
// One real AI product, built with AI coding tools and launched to real users. The AI writes the UI. Your part is the
// engineer's part: ADRs, reviewing every AI-written change, the data model, the AI features, evals, measurements, launch.
// Row format: the plan's "Content row format". A v2 PROJECT id keeps its v2 `url` where the step's content still fits it.
// `basics: true` marks the 6 product basics (◆ in the spec). `after` is the first item of the AI-core slot that must come
// first. New URLs checked 2026-11-15.

export const FLAGSHIP = [
  // ---------- Week 1: pick, brief, research, design docs ----------
  { id: 'proj:pick-scaffold',
    understand: 'Pick the product. The default is the AI workspace from the v2 brief: notes with real-time collaboration and "Ask your workspace" (questions answered from your own notes, with citations). You may pick another product, as long as it has real users in reach, a real-time or data-heavy core, and an AI feature at its centre. Read the Next.js installation guide.',
    url: 'https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/installation', url2: 'https://vercel.com/docs/getting-started-with-vercel',
    do: 'Write ADR 0001: the product you picked (the AI workspace, or another product), who it is for, and why. Then have your AI coding tool scaffold a Next.js + TypeScript app and deploy a hello-world page to a preview URL. Review every file it wrote: delete what you do not need and note what you would have done differently.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what the product is and who uses it, in two sentences. Deep enough when you can say why this product, and what would make you switch to another one in week 2.' },
  { id: 'proj:brief-wireframes', basics: true,
    understand: 'Product basics: the product brief. Target user, the problem, 3 core jobs, success metrics, and what you will not build. Then wireframes for the 3 jobs. Read the Figma guide to auto layout, so the wireframes resize like real screens.',
    url: 'https://help.figma.com/hc/en-us/articles/360040451373',
    do: 'Write a one-page brief in the repo (docs/brief.md). Tear down 2 tools your user already has (for the AI workspace: Notion and Linear docs) and note what they do badly for your user. Have the AI draft wireframes for the 3 core jobs, then edit them until every screen serves one job.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the brief, as you would pitch it to a hiring manager. Deep enough when you can name the one metric that tells you the product works, and why it is not page views.' },
  { id: 'proj:user-research', basics: true,
    understand: 'Product basics: user research in 30 minutes. Talk to 3 people who have the problem. Ask about the last time it happened, not about your idea. Read the NN/g guide to interviewing users.',
    url: 'https://www.nngroup.com/articles/interviewing-users/',
    do: 'Write 5 open questions about the problem (no leading questions, nothing about features). Run 3 short calls or chats of 10 minutes each. Write one note per person: what they do today, what hurts, what they pay for. Change the brief where the research says you were wrong.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what you learned that changed the brief. Deep enough when you can say why "would you use this?" is a bad question, and what you asked instead.' },
  { id: 'proj:design-doc',
    understand: 'Design doc 1: architecture and data model. Context, goals and non-goals, the system diagram, the entities and how they relate. This is the document you steer the AI with. Read Design docs at Google.',
    url: 'https://www.industrialempathy.com/posts/design-docs-at-google/',
    do: 'Write part 1 of docs/design.md: goals and non-goals, an architecture diagram (client, server, database, real-time server, AI services), and the data model (workspaces, members, documents, blocks, and the AI tables to come) with keys and indexes. Ask the AI to find holes in it, and answer each one.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: walk through the diagram and one request end to end. Deep enough when you can say which table grows fastest and how you will query it.' },
  { id: 'proj:design-doc-api',
    understand: 'Design doc 2: the API, the real-time approach, the AI design, and your first ADRs. An ADR is one decision, its context and its consequences, in one short file. Read the ADR site.',
    url: 'https://adr.github.io/', url2: 'https://cognitect.com/blog/2011/11/15/documenting-architecture-decisions',
    do: 'Write part 2 of the design doc: the main API routes with request and response types in TypeScript, the real-time approach (CRDT or server-ordered, with the trade-off), the AI design (what is retrieved, which model, how you will evaluate it), risks and milestones. Write ADRs 0002–0004 for the three biggest choices.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: your real-time choice and what you gave up. Deep enough when you can say what would make you reverse one of your ADRs.' },
  // ---------- Week 2: setup, auth, editor, metrics ----------
  { id: 'proj:setup',
    understand: 'Review the AI scaffold: lint, type checks, tests, CI and preview deploys on every pull request. Write the rules your AI coding agent must follow. Read the Next.js docs on project structure and testing.',
    url: 'https://nextjs.org/docs',
    do: 'Have the AI add ESLint, strict TypeScript, a test runner, a CI workflow and preview deploys. Review the diff line by line. Then write CONTRIBUTING.md for your AI agent: where code goes, how to name things, "every change ships with a test", and what it must never touch. Open one pull request and check CI blocks a failing test.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what your CI checks and why each check is there. Deep enough when you can say which mistake of the AI each rule in CONTRIBUTING.md is meant to stop.' },
  { id: 'proj:auth',
    understand: 'Auth, part 1: sign-in with OAuth or OIDC, sessions, workspaces and roles (owner, editor, viewer). Read the Supabase Auth guide (or your auth provider\'s).',
    url: 'https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth',
    do: 'Write the roles table: what each role may do to a workspace, a document and a member. Have the AI build sign-in, workspace creation and invites. Review where the role is checked on the server, and write tests: a viewer cannot edit, a removed member loses access at once.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: how a request proves who it is and which workspace it may touch. Deep enough when you can say why a role check in the UI alone is not security.' },
  { id: 'proj:auth-rls',
    understand: 'Auth, part 2: the Postgres schema with row-level security, so the database itself stops one workspace from reading another. Read the Supabase row-level security guide.',
    url: 'https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security',
    do: 'Write the schema migration yourself: tables, foreign keys, indexes, and one RLS policy per table keyed on workspace membership. Write a TypeScript test that signs in as user A and tries to read, update and delete user B\'s document; every call must fail. Run EXPLAIN on the main list query with RLS on.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: how your policy decides who sees a row. Deep enough when you can name one query that bypasses RLS and where your code uses it.' },
  { id: 'proj:editor',
    understand: 'The editor: the document model (blocks, marks, a schema), slash commands, and keyboard-first editing. Read the Tiptap docs on the schema and extensions.',
    url: 'https://tiptap.dev/docs',
    do: 'Decide the document model in an ADR: how blocks are stored, what the JSON looks like, how you will migrate it later. Have the AI build the editor with slash commands and a keyboard map. Review its code for state bugs, and write tests for save and load of every block type.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: your document model and why it stores blocks, not one big HTML string. Deep enough when you can say how you would add a new block type without breaking old documents.' },
  { id: 'proj:metrics-analytics', basics: true,
    understand: 'Product basics: metrics and analytics. Pick the events that map to your success metric, name them well, and send them from the server where you can. Read the PostHog product analytics docs.',
    url: 'https://posthog.com/docs/product-analytics',
    do: 'Write a tracking plan (docs/metrics.md): 8–10 events with names, properties and the question each answers. Have the AI add them with PostHog (or similar). Check each event fires once, with the right properties, in a test or the live view. Build a first dashboard for activation.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: your activation event and why it means a user got value. Deep enough when you can say which event would mislead you if it fired twice.' },
  // ---------- Week 3: real-time and offline ----------
  { id: 'proj:realtime',
    understand: 'Real-time collaboration, part 1: the CRDT model. How Yjs represents a document, how updates merge, and the WebSocket provider. Read the Yjs docs introduction.',
    url: 'https://docs.yjs.dev/',
    do: 'Write an ADR on how the editor state maps to a Yjs document and where updates are stored. Have the AI wire Yjs to the editor with a WebSocket provider. Review it, then test it: two browser windows type in the same block at once and both end with the same text.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: what happens when two people type in the same block at once. Deep enough when you can say why every client ends up with the same document without a central lock.' },
  { id: 'proj:realtime-presence',
    understand: 'Real-time collaboration, part 2: presence (who is here, live cursors) and reconnect. Presence is short-lived state, kept apart from the document. Read the Yjs awareness guide.',
    url: 'https://docs.yjs.dev/getting-started/adding-awareness',
    do: 'Have the AI add live cursors and an avatar list with Yjs awareness. Review how presence is cleaned up when a tab closes. Then cut the network for 30 seconds in DevTools while typing in both windows, reconnect, and write down what the user sees. File and fix the worst issue.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: why presence is not stored with the document. Deep enough when you can say what your app shows during a reconnect, and what it should show.' },
  { id: 'proj:local-first-software',
    understand: 'The local-first read: Ink & Switch, "Local-first software" (Kleppmann, Wiggins, van Hardenberg, McGranaghan, 2019). The seven ideals, why CRDTs, and what is still hard.',
    url: 'https://www.inkandswitch.com/essay/local-first/',
    do: 'Score your product against the seven ideals, 0–2 each, with one line of evidence each. Pick the two ideals that matter most to your user and write what the next two steps (offline-first) must do to reach them.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: what happens when two people edit offline and reconnect. Deep enough when you can say how a CRDT merges their changes, and one case where the merge is correct but surprising.' },
  { id: 'proj:offline',
    understand: 'Offline-first, part 1: persist the document in IndexedDB, keep editing offline, and sync on reconnect. Read the Yjs guide to offline editing.',
    url: 'https://docs.yjs.dev/getting-started/allowing-offline-editing',
    do: 'Decide in an ADR what works offline (editing, search, AI?) and what does not. Have the AI add IndexedDB persistence and an offline banner. Review it, then go offline, edit, reload the tab, and check nothing is lost.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what is stored on the device and when it syncs. Deep enough when you can say what a user loses if they clear site data while offline.' },
  { id: 'proj:offline-tests',
    understand: 'Offline-first, part 2: conflict and reconnect tests. Two clients edit offline and reconnect; the test proves they converge. Read the Yjs document-updates API.',
    url: 'https://docs.yjs.dev/api/document-updates',
    do: 'Write TypeScript tests with two Yjs documents and no network: both edit the same block, exchange updates in both orders, and must end equal. Add a test for a delete that races an edit. Add the reconnect UX the tests showed you need (a "synced" state, a conflict note).',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: what your conflict tests prove and what they do not. Deep enough when you can say why the order the updates arrive in does not change the result.' },
  // ---------- Week 4: search, performance, accessibility, first AI feature ----------
  { id: 'proj:search-performance',
    understand: 'Search and a ⌘K command palette with virtualized results: only the rows on screen are rendered. Read the TanStack Virtual docs.',
    url: 'https://tanstack.com/virtual/latest', url2: 'https://github.com/dip/cmdk',
    do: 'Decide where search runs (Postgres full-text, or on the client) in an ADR, with numbers for 10,000 documents. Have the AI build the ⌘K palette with keyboard navigation and a virtualized list. Seed 10,000 documents and measure the time from key press to results.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: why virtualization keeps a long list fast. Deep enough when you can say what breaks with virtualization (find-in-page, screen readers, row heights) and how you handled it.' },
  { id: 'proj:perf-pass',
    understand: 'Performance pass: a Web Vitals budget (LCP, INP, CLS), code splitting, and the React Profiler. Measure first, then fix the biggest cost. Read web.dev on Web Vitals.',
    url: 'https://web.dev/articles/vitals', url2: 'https://react.dev/reference/react/Profiler',
    do: 'Set a budget in docs/performance.md. Record a Lighthouse run and a React Profiler trace of typing in a long document. Find the 2 biggest costs, ask the AI for fixes, review them, and record before and after numbers.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: your INP before and after, and what caused the slow part. Deep enough when you can say why a re-render on every keystroke is slow and how you proved your fix worked.' },
  { id: 'proj:a11y',
    understand: 'Accessibility pass: keyboard only, a screen reader, contrast, and axe in CI. Read the axe-core README, then the ARIA patterns for the widgets you use.',
    url: 'https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core', url2: 'https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/',
    do: 'Use the product for 15 minutes with the keyboard only, then with VoiceOver or NVDA. List every place you got stuck. Have the AI fix them, review each fix against the ARIA pattern, and add axe to CI so a regression fails the build.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the worst accessibility bug you found and why the AI wrote it. Deep enough when you can say what axe cannot catch and how you test that by hand.' },
  { id: 'proj:ai-assistant', covers: ['aib:ai-sdk-react'], after: 'aib:stream-tokens-react',
    understand: 'The first AI feature: a streaming assistant in the editor with the Vercel AI SDK (useChat, streaming, stop and retry). Read the AI SDK introduction.',
    url: 'https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/introduction',
    do: 'Write the feature spec: what the assistant does (rewrite, summarize, continue), what it sees, and what it costs per call. Have the AI build it with the AI SDK. Review the server route: the API key stays on the server, input size is capped, and errors reach the UI. Log tokens and latency for every call.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what happens from the key press to the first token on screen. Deep enough when you can say what the user sees when the model times out halfway through an answer.' },
  { id: 'proj:ab-tests', basics: true,
    understand: 'Product basics: A/B tests and the stats behind them. Sample size, why peeking at results misleads, and when a result is real. Read Evan Miller, "How not to run an A/B test".',
    url: 'https://www.evanmiller.org/how-not-to-run-an-ab-test.html', url2: 'https://www.evanmiller.org/ab-testing/sample-size.html',
    do: 'Write a test plan for the assistant: the hypothesis, the metric, the minimum effect you care about, and the sample size from Evan Miller\'s calculator. In a TS scratch file, simulate 1,000 A/A tests where you peek every day and count how often you would have "won".',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: why peeking raises false positives. Deep enough when you can say how many users your test needs, and what you will do if you never get that many.' },
  // ---------- Week 5: tools and retrieval ----------
  { id: 'proj:ai-tools', after: 'aib:tool-call-loop',
    understand: 'Tools in the product: let the assistant call your own functions (search documents, read a document, list tasks). Read the AI SDK guide to tools and tool calling.',
    url: 'https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/ai-sdk-core/tools-and-tool-calling',
    do: 'Define 3 read-only tools with typed input schemas. Have the AI wire them in. Review every tool for permission checks: a tool must only see what the user can see. Write tests for a bad argument, a tool that throws, and a loop that calls tools forever (cap the steps).',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what the model sees of a tool and what your server checks. Deep enough when you can say why every tool runs with the user\'s permissions, not the server\'s.' },
  { id: 'proj:ai-ingestion', after: 'aib:embeddings-neighbours',
    understand: 'Ingestion, part 1: chunk and embed on save. Split documents into chunks, embed each, store them in Postgres with pgvector. Read the pgvector README.',
    url: 'https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector',
    do: 'Write the chunk table yourself (document id, workspace id, chunk text, embedding, content hash, model name). Choose a chunk size in an ADR. Have the AI add a background job that embeds on save. Review it, and check that saving twice without changes does not embed twice.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: how a document becomes searchable chunks. Deep enough when you can say why you store the model name and a content hash on every chunk.' },
  { id: 'proj:ai-reindex', after: 'aib:pgvector',
    understand: 'Ingestion, part 2: re-index on edit or on a model change, so no stale chunk ends up in an answer. Read Supabase on automatic embeddings.',
    url: 'https://supabase.com/docs/guides/ai/automatic-embeddings',
    do: 'Re-embed only the chunks whose hash changed when a document is edited, and delete chunks of deleted documents. Write a backfill script for a model change that runs in batches and can resume. Write a test: edit a document, ask about the old text, and check no old chunk comes back.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: how a stale chunk ends up in an answer and how your re-indexing stops it. Deep enough when you can say how long a model change takes for 100,000 chunks, and what users see meanwhile.' },
  { id: 'proj:hybrid-search', after: 'aib:retrieval-chunking-hybrid',
    understand: 'Hybrid search and reranking: combine keyword search and vector search, then rerank the top results. Read the Supabase guide to hybrid search.',
    url: 'https://supabase.com/docs/guides/ai/hybrid-search',
    do: 'Write 20 test questions over your own seed data, each with the document that should come back. Measure recall@5 for vector only, keyword only, and hybrid with reciprocal rank fusion. Add a reranker if it helps. Keep the numbers in docs/retrieval.md.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: when keyword search beats vectors. Deep enough when you can show one of your questions that only hybrid gets right, and say why.' },
  { id: 'proj:ai-ask-workspace', after: 'ai:rag-paper',
    understand: 'Ask your workspace, part 1: answers with citations. Retrieve chunks, answer only from them, and link each claim to its source. Read the Claude citations guide.',
    url: 'https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations',
    do: 'Write the prompt and the answer format: every sentence cites a chunk, and "I do not know" is allowed. Have the AI build the Ask panel with streaming and clickable citations. Review it. Ask 10 questions and mark each answer right, wrong, or right with a wrong citation.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: the path from question to cited answer. Deep enough when you can say how a wrong citation happens and which log shows you the cause.' },
  // ---------- Week 6: permissions, research, evals ----------
  { id: 'proj:ai-ask-permissions',
    understand: 'Ask your workspace, part 2: permission-aware retrieval. Retrieval must only return chunks the asking user may read, even when sharing changes. Read Supabase on RAG with permissions.',
    url: 'https://supabase.com/docs/guides/ai/rag-with-permissions',
    do: 'Filter chunks by workspace and document access inside the database query, not after it. Write tests: user B asks about user A\'s private document and gets nothing; access is removed and the next answer no longer uses it. Check the filter does not wreck recall@5.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: how a leaked answer would happen in your design. Deep enough when you can say why filtering after retrieval is not enough.' },
  { id: 'proj:user-research-ai',
    understand: 'A second round of user research, on the AI feature. Watch people use Ask your workspace on their own notes, without help. Read the NN/g guide to usability testing.',
    url: 'https://www.nngroup.com/articles/usability-testing-101/',
    do: 'Run 3 sessions of 15 minutes: give each person 3 tasks and stay quiet while they work. Note where they hesitate, what they expected, and every answer they did not trust. Turn the notes into 5 failure cases for next step\'s eval set.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the moment a user stopped trusting the AI. Deep enough when you can say what you will change, and how you will know it worked.' },
  { id: 'proj:evals', after: 'aib:evals-test-cases',
    understand: 'The product\'s eval set, built from real failures: questions from users, the failures you saw, and what a good answer must contain. Read Hamel Husain, "Your AI product needs evals".',
    url: 'https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/',
    do: 'Build 50 eval cases in a JSON file: question, workspace fixture, expected source, and the facts a good answer must contain. Write a TypeScript runner that scores retrieval and answers with code checks first. Run it and save a baseline.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: how a failing eval leads you to the cause. Deep enough when you can say which of your cases came from a real user, and why those matter most.' },
  { id: 'proj:llm-judge', after: 'aib:evals-llm-judge',
    understand: 'An LLM judge, checked against your labels. Use a model to grade answers where code cannot, and measure how often it agrees with you. Read Hamel Husain on LLM-as-a-judge.',
    url: 'https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/llm-judge/',
    do: 'Label 30 answers yourself as pass or fail, with a reason. Write a judge prompt with one pass/fail question, run it on the same 30, and measure agreement. Fix the prompt until it agrees on at least 25. Add it to the runner for the cases code cannot check.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: where your judge disagrees with you and why. Deep enough when you can say why you check the judge against your own labels before you trust it.' },
  { id: 'proj:eval-gate', after: 'fde:eval-pipelines',
    understand: 'An eval gate in CI, plus traces. A pull request that changes a prompt, a model or retrieval must not lower the eval score. Read the promptfoo GitHub Action guide.',
    url: 'https://www.promptfoo.dev/docs/integrations/github-action/',
    do: 'Run the eval set in CI on every pull request that touches AI code, and fail below the baseline. Post the score diff as a comment. Add a trace id to every AI call, so a failing eval case links to its trace. Break a prompt on purpose and check the gate stops it.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what your gate checks and what it costs to run. Deep enough when you can say how you keep the gate from becoming flaky.' },
  // ---------- Week 7 (after the take-home): agent actions ----------
  { id: 'proj:ai-agent-actions', after: 'aib:react-build-agent',
    understand: 'Agent actions, part 1: the assistant acts, not just answers (summarize a document, create tasks from notes). Read Anthropic, "Building effective agents".',
    url: 'https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents',
    do: 'Pick 2 actions and write each as a workflow or an agent, with the reason, in an ADR. Have the AI build them with write tools. Review the loop: step cap, timeout, and what happens when a tool fails. Add 10 eval cases for the actions.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: what the loop does when a tool call fails or returns nonsense. Deep enough when you can say why you chose a workflow or an agent for each action.' },
  { id: 'proj:ai-agent-permissions', after: 'aib:agents-sdk-build',
    understand: 'Agent actions, part 2: human confirmation and tool permissions. Every write is shown to the user and approved before it runs. Read the AI SDK guide to tool usage in chat.',
    url: 'https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/ai-sdk-ui/chatbot-tool-usage',
    do: 'Split tools into read and write. Write tools pause and show a diff the user approves or rejects. Log every approved action with the user, tool and arguments, and add undo. Write a test where a document tells the agent to delete everything, and check nothing runs without approval.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: where the human step stops damage. Deep enough when you can say which action you would let run without approval, and why.' },
  // ---------- Week 8: observability, abuse, integrations, rollout ----------
  { id: 'proj:observability-security',
    understand: 'Observability, part 1: tracing every AI call, and a cost and latency dashboard. Read the Langfuse docs.',
    url: 'https://langfuse.com/docs',
    do: 'Trace every AI call with inputs, outputs, model, tokens, cost and latency (Langfuse or OpenTelemetry). Build a dashboard: p50 and p95 latency, cost per user per day, error rate. Set one alert. Find the most expensive call and write down why.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what one trace shows for a bad answer. Deep enough when you can say your cost per active user and what drives it.' },
  { id: 'proj:rate-limits',
    understand: 'Observability, part 2: rate limits and abuse controls, so one user or a script cannot run up your bill. Read the Upstash ratelimit README.',
    url: 'https://github.com/upstash/ratelimit-js',
    do: 'Set limits per user and per workspace for each AI route, plus a daily spend cap. Return a clear error and show it in the UI. Write a test script that sends 200 requests and check the limit holds. Write down what you do when a user hits the cap.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: your limits and how you picked them. Deep enough when you can say how a sliding window differs from a fixed window, and which one you used.' },
  { id: 'proj:integrations',
    understand: 'Integrations, part 1: import. Bring a user\'s existing notes in (Markdown files, CSV, or a Notion export). This is the forward-deployed muscle: other people\'s messy data. Read the Notion API quick start.',
    url: 'https://developers.notion.com/guides/notion-api/quick-start',
    do: 'Map the import format to your document model in a table, including what you drop. Have the AI build the importer as a background job. Test it on 3 real exports, including a broken one. Check imported documents are chunked and searchable.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the worst thing you found in real export data. Deep enough when you can say how a half-failed import looks to the user and how they retry it.' },
  { id: 'proj:webhooks-out',
    understand: 'Integrations, part 2: outgoing webhooks. Tell other systems when something changes, signed, retried and safe to receive twice. Read the Standard Webhooks spec.',
    url: 'https://www.standardwebhooks.com/',
    do: 'Design the event payloads with TypeScript types. Sign each delivery, retry with backoff, and keep a delivery log the user can see. Write a small receiver that verifies the signature and drops duplicates by event id. Test a receiver that is down for 10 minutes.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: why the receiver must handle the same event twice. Deep enough when you can say how a receiver knows a webhook really came from you.' },
  { id: 'proj:flag-rollout', basics: true,
    understand: 'Product basics: roll out the assistant behind a feature flag and read the result. Ship to a slice of users, compare, then widen or roll back. Read the PostHog feature flags docs.',
    url: 'https://posthog.com/docs/feature-flags', url2: 'https://martinfowler.com/articles/feature-toggles.html',
    do: 'Put Ask your workspace behind a flag for half your users. Pick the success metric and the guardrail metric (errors, cost) before you look. After a few days, read both, write the decision in docs/experiments.md, and remove the flag or roll back.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what the rollout showed and what you decided. Deep enough when you can say why you picked the guardrail metric before you saw any data.' },
  // ---------- Week 9: cost, routing, load and failure ----------
  { id: 'proj:finetune-vs-rag', after: 'ai:fine-tune-evaluate',
    understand: 'Fine-tune vs RAG for one feature, with numbers. Fine-tuning changes how a model answers; RAG changes what it knows. Read Hamel Husain, "Is fine-tuning still valuable?".',
    url: 'https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/fine_tuning_valuable.html',
    do: 'Pick one feature (for example, titles or tags for a document). Compare a prompt with retrieved examples against a small model fine-tuned through a provider\'s fine-tuning API, on your eval cases: quality, latency and cost per 1,000 calls. Write an ADR with the numbers and your choice.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: which one you chose and why. Deep enough when you can say what change in your data would flip the decision.' },
  { id: 'proj:model-routing', after: 'ai:prompt-caching-cost',
    understand: 'Model routing: send easy calls to a cheaper, faster model and keep the strong model for hard ones. Read the RouteLLM paper abstract and results.',
    url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18665',
    do: 'Sort your AI calls by task and pick a model for each. Add a simple router (by task, then by input length). Run the eval set per route and check the cheap route holds its score. Record cost and p95 latency before and after.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: your routing rule and what it saved. Deep enough when you can say how you would catch the cheap model getting worse after a provider update.' },
  { id: 'proj:caching',
    after: 'ai:prompt-caching-cost',
    understand: 'Prompt and semantic caching: reuse the stable part of a prompt, and reuse answers for repeat questions. Read the Claude prompt caching guide.',
    url: 'https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching',
    do: 'Order your prompts so the stable part comes first, turn on prompt caching, and measure cached tokens. Add a semantic cache for Ask with a similarity threshold and a workspace key. Find the threshold where a wrong cached answer appears, and set yours below it.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what each cache saves and what it can get wrong. Deep enough when you can say why the semantic cache must be keyed by workspace and invalidated on edit.' },
  { id: 'proj:load-failure',
    understand: 'Load test: find the point where the product slows down, before your users do. Read the k6 docs.',
    url: 'https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/',
    do: 'Write a k6 script in TypeScript: sign in, open a document, type, ask a question. Ramp to 10× your expected users. Record p95 latency and errors per route, find the first bottleneck (database, real-time server, AI provider), fix it, and run again.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what broke first and why. Deep enough when you can say how many users your setup holds today, and the next limit after the fix.' },
  { id: 'proj:failure-test',
    understand: 'Failure test: kill the WebSocket server, slow the AI provider, drop the database, and fix what breaks. Read the Principles of Chaos Engineering.',
    url: 'https://principlesofchaos.org/',
    do: 'Write down what should happen first, then: kill the real-time server while 2 clients type, add 10 s of latency to the AI provider, and restart the database. Note what users actually saw. Fix the worst gap (timeouts, retries, a clear error) and add a test for it.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: the failure that surprised you. Deep enough when you can say what a user loses in each failure, and what you changed so they lose less.' },
  // ---------- Week 10 (after the take-home): guardrails and the gateway ----------
  { id: 'proj:guardrails', after: 'ai:owasp-llm',
    understand: 'Guardrails and prompt-injection tests. Your documents are untrusted input: a note can carry instructions to the model. Read OWASP LLM01, Prompt Injection.',
    url: 'https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/', url2: 'https://simonwillison.net/2023/Apr/14/worst-that-can-happen/',
    do: 'Write 15 attack cases into the eval set: injected notes, data-exfiltration links, tool misuse, and leaks across workspaces. Run them, fix what gets through (output checks, tool limits, no auto-loaded links), and keep them in the CI gate.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: the attack that worked and how you stopped it. Deep enough when you can say why prompt injection cannot be fully solved in the prompt, and what limits the damage instead.' },
  { id: 'proj:llm-gateway', after: 'aib:llm-gateway',
    understand: 'Put an LLM gateway in the product: one place for keys, retries, fallbacks, limits, caching and logs across providers. Read the Cloudflare AI Gateway docs.',
    url: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/',
    do: 'Route every AI call through one gateway module (your own, or a hosted one). Add a fallback provider, a timeout and a retry budget. Turn the main provider off and check the product keeps working on the fallback. Move cost logging there.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what the gateway owns and what stays in the feature code. Deep enough when you can say what happens to cost and quality when the fallback takes over.' },
  // ---------- Week 11: launch ----------
  { id: 'proj:launch-checklist',
    understand: 'The launch checklist: what must be true before real users arrive. Backups, alerts, limits, a support channel, a rollback plan. Read the Google SRE chapter on reliable product launches.',
    url: 'https://sre.google/sre-book/reliable-product-launches/',
    do: 'Write docs/launch.md: a checklist of 15–20 items across reliability, security, privacy, cost and support, each with an owner (you) and proof. Restore a backup to check it works. Do a rollback drill on the preview deploy.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the riskiest item on your list. Deep enough when you can say what you do in the first 10 minutes if the launch goes wrong.' },
  { id: 'proj:launch',
    understand: 'Launch: get 5–10 real users. Invite them one by one and onboard them yourself. Read Paul Graham, "Do things that don\'t scale".',
    url: 'https://www.paulgraham.com/ds.html',
    do: 'Deploy to production. Write a short invite and send it to 15–20 people who have the problem. Onboard each user on a call or in chat, and watch the activation event in your dashboard. Keep a log of every bug and question.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: who signed up and who got value. Deep enough when you can say what share of users reached activation, and where the rest stopped.' },
  { id: 'proj:user-interviews',
    understand: 'Interview your real users: what they used, what they ignored, and what they would miss. Read the YC guide to talking to users.',
    url: 'https://www.ycombinator.com/library/Iq-how-to-talk-to-users',
    do: 'Interview 3–5 users for 15 minutes each. Ask about what they did, not what they think. Pair each interview with their events in your analytics. Write the top 3 problems, ranked by how many users hit them.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what users said compared with what they did. Deep enough when you can name one thing a user said they wanted that the data says they do not use.' },
  { id: 'proj:launch-fix',
    understand: 'Ship one fix from the feedback. Pick the problem that costs the most users, fix it, and tell them. Read Intercom on RICE prioritization.',
    url: 'https://www.intercom.com/blog/rice-simple-prioritization-for-product-managers',
    do: 'Score the top 3 problems with RICE. Write the fix as a short spec, have the AI build it, review it, and ship it with a test. Message the users who reported it. Check the metric it should move a few days later.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what you fixed and why that one first. Deep enough when you can say how you will know the fix worked.' },
  { id: 'proj:funnel-retention', basics: true,
    understand: 'Product basics: read the funnel and retention. Where users drop between sign-up and value, and how many come back. Read the PostHog retention docs.',
    url: 'https://posthog.com/docs/product-analytics/retention', url2: 'https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/what-is-good-retention-issue-29',
    do: 'Build the sign-up → activation funnel and a weekly retention table for your users. Write a one-page read in docs/metrics.md: the biggest drop, a guess at why, and the evidence from your interviews. Pick the one change you will test next.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: your funnel and retention, with the numbers. Deep enough when you can say why retention matters more than sign-ups, and what is a good number for a tool like yours.' },
  // ---------- Week 12: iterate, refactor, one more AI feature ----------
  { id: 'proj:data-iteration',
    understand: 'One data-driven iteration behind a flag: a change chosen from the funnel read, shipped to some users, and measured. Read the PostHog experiments docs.',
    url: 'https://posthog.com/docs/experiments',
    do: 'Write the hypothesis and the metric first. Ship the change behind a flag to half your users. Read the result, honest about your small sample, and write the decision and what you learned in docs/experiments.md.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the change, the result, and the decision. Deep enough when you can say what you can and cannot conclude from 10 users.' },
  { id: 'proj:adr-revisit',
    understand: 'Revisit 3 ADRs with what you learned. A decision record is most useful when you mark what turned out wrong. Read the ADR examples and templates on GitHub.',
    url: 'https://github.com/architecture-decision-record/architecture-decision-record',
    do: 'Pick 3 early ADRs (real-time, data model, AI design). For each, add a "What happened" section: what was right, what was wrong, and what it cost. Supersede one with a new ADR if you would now decide differently.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: the decision you got most wrong. Deep enough when you can say what you knew then, what you know now, and how you would have found out sooner.' },
  { id: 'proj:refactor-review',
    understand: 'Review AI-written code for smells and refactor one module. AI code tends to repeat itself, grow long functions and add dead options. Read the Refactoring Guru catalogue of code smells.',
    url: 'https://refactoring.guru/refactoring/smells',
    do: 'Pick the module you trust least. List its smells with line references (duplication, long functions, wrong names, dead code, missing tests). Add tests first, then refactor in small commits, with the AI doing the edits and you reviewing each one. Record lines and test coverage before and after.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the smells you found and which one the AI made most. Deep enough when you can say how you knew the refactor did not change behaviour.' },
  { id: 'proj:multimodal', after: 'aib:multimodal-upload',
    understand: 'A multimodal or in-browser AI feature: ask about an uploaded image or screenshot, or run a small model in the browser. Read the Claude vision guide.',
    url: 'https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/vision', url2: 'https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/index',
    do: 'Pick one: image upload into Ask your workspace, or an in-browser model (for example, embeddings or tagging with Transformers.js). Write the spec with size and cost limits. Have the AI build it, review uploads for type and size checks, and add 5 eval cases.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what the feature does and what it costs. Deep enough when you can say when running a model in the browser beats calling an API.' },
  { id: 'proj:eval-cost-report',
    understand: 'An eval regression and cost report, published. Show how quality, latency and cost moved over the project. Read Eugene Yan, "Patterns for building LLM-based systems".',
    url: 'https://eugeneyan.com/writing/llm-patterns',
    do: 'Run the full eval set on 3 points in your history (first Ask, after hybrid search, today). Make a table of score, p95 latency and cost per 1,000 calls. Write a one-page report with what moved each number, and publish it in the repo or on your site.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: the change that helped quality most, and what it cost. Deep enough when you can defend every number in the report.' },
  // ---------- Week 13: case study, present, retro ----------
  { id: 'proj:case-study',
    understand: 'Case study, part 1: the README, an architecture diagram and the trade-offs. Written for a hiring manager who has 5 minutes. Read the C4 model for architecture diagrams.',
    url: 'https://c4model.com/',
    do: 'Write the README: the problem, the users, a context and container diagram, the 5 biggest trade-offs with links to ADRs, and the metrics (users, retention, eval score, cost). Ask the AI to read it as a hiring manager and list what is unclear, then fix it.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: the project in the order of the README. Deep enough when you can say which trade-off you are proudest of and which one you would undo.' },
  { id: 'proj:demo-video',
    understand: 'Case study, part 2: a 3-minute demo video. Show the problem, the 3 core jobs and one hard part, not every feature. Use a free screen recorder such as OBS.',
    url: 'https://obsproject.com/',
    do: 'Write a script: 20 s problem, 2 min product, 40 s one hard part (real-time or retrieval). Record it in one take per section, cut it to 3 minutes, and add it to the README. Ask 2 people to watch it and tell you what they remember.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: give the demo without the video. Deep enough when you can show the hard part and say why it was hard in under a minute.' },
  { id: 'proj:deep-dive',
    understand: 'Present the product as a deep-dive interview: 15 minutes on the project, then follow-up questions on design, trade-offs and what went wrong. Read the Tech Interview Handbook on behavioural interviews.',
    url: 'https://www.techinterviewhandbook.org/behavioral-interview/',
    do: 'Write 10 follow-up questions an interviewer would ask (why this database, how do you know the AI is good, what broke). Answer each in 2 sentences with a number. Record yourself giving the 15-minute deep dive and answering 5 of them, then fix the weakest answer.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 15 min: the full deep dive. Deep enough when you can answer "what would you do differently" with a real decision and its cost.' },
  { id: 'proj:final-iteration',
    understand: 'A final iteration from user feedback. Watch real sessions, find one friction point, and remove it. Read the PostHog session replay docs.',
    url: 'https://posthog.com/docs/session-replay',
    do: 'Watch 5 session replays (with users\' consent) and list the friction points. Pick one, write the change, have the AI build it, review it and ship it. Tell the users who hit it.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 2 min: what you saw in the replays and what you changed. Deep enough when you can say what you would build next and why, from the data.' },
  { id: 'proj:retro',
    understand: 'Retro, and the roadmap after the plan. What went well, what did not, and what the product does next. Read the Atlassian retrospective play.',
    url: 'https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook/plays/retrospective',
    do: 'Write docs/retro.md: what went well, what did not, what you learned about working with AI coding tools, and 3 things you would do differently. Then write the next 3 months of the product roadmap, or a handover note if you stop here.',
    explain: 'Out loud, 3 min: the retro, as you would tell it in an interview. Deep enough when you can say what you now do differently when an AI writes your code.' },
];

// Product engineer take-home simulations (v3 §5, moved from mc.js): weeks 7 and 10, Mon–Wed (3 × 90 min plus optional
// Sunday time). Written in the style of the Linear and PostHog take-homes. The brief's link is on part 1. URLs checked 2026-11-15.
export const TAKE_HOMES = [
  {
    id: 'mc:take-home-issue-triage', week: 7, style: 'Linear',
    text: 'Take-home (Linear style, 4–8 h): build an issue triage inbox. Seed 200 issues (title, status, priority, assignee, labels, created). The inbox lists them with filters and sort; you triage with the keyboard only: j/k to move, Enter to open a detail pane, s/p/a to set status, priority and assignee, e to archive, ⌘K for a command menu, and undo for the last action. Changes are optimistic and survive a reload. It must be polished, keyboard-first and accessible (focus management, ARIA roles, screen-reader announcements, visible focus). Hand in a README of trade-offs (what you cut and why, what you would do next) and a recorded walkthrough of 5 minutes or less.',
    parts: [
      { id: 'mc:take-home-issue-triage-1', text: 'Take-home 1/3 (issue triage inbox): read the brief, write a half-page plan (data model, state, keyboard map, what you will cut), scaffold, seed data, the list with filters and sort.', url: 'https://linear.app/docs/conceptual-model', mins: 90 },
      { id: 'mc:take-home-issue-triage-2', text: 'Take-home 2/3 (issue triage inbox): the keyboard layer (j/k, Enter, s/p/a, e, ⌘K, undo), the detail pane, optimistic updates persisted across reloads, focus management and screen-reader announcements.', mins: 90 },
      { id: 'mc:take-home-issue-triage-3', text: 'Take-home 3/3 (issue triage inbox): polish (empty and loading states, motion, contrast), an axe pass and a few tests, the README of trade-offs, and the recorded walkthrough. Use optional Sunday time to finish; stop at 8 h.', mins: 90 },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'mc:take-home-funnel-explorer', week: 10, style: 'PostHog',
    text: 'Take-home (PostHog style, 4–8 h): build a product-analytics funnel explorer. Generate 50,000 events (user, event name, timestamp, properties). The user picks 2–5 steps and a date range and sees the conversion funnel: counts and drop-off per step, a breakdown by one property, and the list of users who dropped at a step. State lives in the URL so a funnel can be shared, and the event table stays fast (virtualized, filtering off the main thread or debounced). It must be polished, keyboard-first and accessible (every control reachable by keyboard, the chart has a table fallback, visible focus). Hand in a README of trade-offs (data shape, where you compute the funnel, what breaks at 10× the data) and a recorded walkthrough of 5 minutes or less.',
    parts: [
      { id: 'mc:take-home-funnel-explorer-1', text: 'Take-home 1/3 (funnel explorer): read the brief, write a half-page plan (event shape, where the funnel is computed, URL state, what you will cut), generate the data, the step picker and date range.', url: 'https://posthog.com/docs/product-analytics/funnels', mins: 90 },
      { id: 'mc:take-home-funnel-explorer-2', text: 'Take-home 2/3 (funnel explorer): the funnel computation and chart with its table fallback, the property breakdown, drop-off users, shareable URL state, and the fast virtualized event table.', mins: 90 },
      { id: 'mc:take-home-funnel-explorer-3', text: 'Take-home 3/3 (funnel explorer): polish, keyboard and screen-reader pass, a few tests on the funnel maths, the README of trade-offs, and the recorded walkthrough. Use optional Sunday time to finish; stop at 8 h.', mins: 90 },
    ],
  },
];

